// @run
// @log 5
// A module GLOBAL annotated with a VALUE UNION and initialized by an OBJECT LITERAL whose
// FIELD-NAME SET matches an interned struct row — here the arm's own nested field type
// (`Outer = {v: Inner}` beside `Inner = {v: i32}`, both `{v}`). The union annotation pins the
// cell to the `{tag, value}` box, so it has no struct-table layout; `structIndexOfLet` used to
// ignore that and fall through to the initializer, which an object literal answers by field-name
// SET, handing back the twin row. `structIndexOfExpr`'s Ident arm then reported that row for `u`
// and its `msi >= 0` branch short-circuited the narrowed-variant resolution the read needs.
//
// THE AXIS IS THE FIELDSET TWIN, NOT THE NESTING and not the identifier `v`. Measured cell by
// cell on the same depth-2 shape, union arm and nested field type written as:
//   - `{v}` / `{v}` — twin — was the reject; `{w}` / `{w}` was too (so not the spelling `v`);
//   - `{v}` / `{v, k}` and `{a, b}` / `{a}` — the SAME "outer field name is also an inner field
//     name" reading, sets DIFFERENT — always worked, before and after;
//   - `{a, b}` / `{b, a}` — twin by set, different declaration order — was the reject;
//   - `{v: string}` under `{v: Inner}` — twin by set, field TYPES different — was the reject.
// The partner row need not be the field's own type at all: an UNRELATED same-fieldset struct
// reproduces it, which is what `union-global-literal-twin-unrelated-struct.vl` pins.
//
// AND THE STORAGE CLASS IS HALF THE AXIS. The identical shape as a PARAM, as a LOCAL, or as a
// global initialized from a CALL rather than a literal all worked throughout — only the
// global-with-literal spelling reaches `structIndexOfLet`'s initializer fall-through. Drop the
// union and it works too (a plain `const u: Outer` resolves by annotation).
//
// SPELLING: the generic `Box<Box<i32>> | i32` reaches this same root and is fixed with it. The
// fully INLINE spelling (`const u: {v: {v: i32}} | i32`) never had it — its annotation is no
// union ALIAS, so it never took the fall-through. `type Outer = { v: { v: i32 } }` (inner shape
// inline) stops earlier still, in the variant-field collector
// (`only i32 / boolean / string / array union-variant fields are supported`) — a different,
// still-open gap that this root never reached.
type Inner = { v: i32 }
type Outer = { v: Inner }
type U = Outer | i32

const u: U = { v: { v: 5 } }
if u is Outer { print(u.v.v) } else { print(0) }
